WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.420
Everybody is familiar with the feeling

00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:04.540
that things are not as they should be.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.620
That you're not successful enough,

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.460
your relationship's not satisfying enough,

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.820
that you don't have the things you crave.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.440
A chronic dissatisfaction

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.900
that makes you look outwards with envy

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.080
and inwards with disappointment.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.240
Pop culture, advertising, and

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.280
social media make this worse

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.360
by reminding you that aiming for anything

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:28.320
less than your dream job is failure,

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:29.260
you need to have great

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:30.720
experiences constantly,

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.260
be conventionally attractive,

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:33.340
have a lot of friends,

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:34.620
and find your soulmate,

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:35.360
and that others

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:36.480
have all of these things

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:37.960
and are truly happy.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:40.120
And, of course, a vast array of

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:41.640
self-improvement products

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.520
implies that it's all your fault for

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.660
not working hard enough on yourself.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:47.960
In the last two decades,

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:49.260
researchers have been starting

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:50.760
to investigate how we can

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.320
counteract these impulses.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.480
The field of positive psychology emerged,

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.860
the study of what makes life worth living,

00:00:57.860 --> 00:00:59.800
while cognitive behavioral therapy

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.020
was developed to change negative feelings.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.360
Scientists began to ask

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:05.700
"Why are some people happier

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.360
and more satisfied than others?"

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.111
"And are there ways to apply what

00:01:09.111 --> 00:01:11.180
they're doing right to the rest of us?"

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.253
In this video, we want to talk about

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:14.720
one of the strongest predictors of

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:16.180
how happy people are,

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:17.700
how easily they make friends,

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:18.840
and how good they are at

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:19.960
dealing with hardship.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.640
An antidote to dissatisfaction, so to speak:

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:25.360
Gratitude.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:27.401
While gratitude may sound like another

00:01:27.401 --> 00:01:28.540
self-improvement trend,

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.380
preached by people
who use hashtags,

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:34.560
what we currently know about it is based
on a body of scientific work and studies.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:36.700
We'll include them in the description.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.900
Gratitude can mean very different things
to different people in different contexts.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:46.480
It's a character trait, a feeling,
a virtue, and a behavior.

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:50.380
You can feel grateful towards someone
who did something for you,

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.740
for random events,
like the weather,

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.020
or even for nature or fate,

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.880
and it's wired into our biology.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:01.380
1: How Gratitude Connects Us to Each Other.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:06.220
The predecessor of gratitude
is probably reciprocity.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.520
It likely evolved as
a biological signal

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:13.120
that motivates animals to exchange
things for their mutual benefit

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:14.740
and can be found
in the animal kingdom

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:17.420
among certain fish,
birds, or mammals,

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:19.180
but especially in primates.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.220
When your brain recognizes that
someone's done something nice for you,

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:26.280
it reacts with gratitude to
motivate you to repay them.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.700
This gratitude makes
you care about others,

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.300
and others care about you.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:33.300
This was important

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.940
because, as human brains got
better at reading emotions,

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.900
selfish individuals were
identified and shunned.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:43.360
It became an evolutionary advantage
to play well with others

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:45.440
and build lasting relationships.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.220
For example,
if you were hungry

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:50.920
and someone else showed
you where to find tasty berries,

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:52.740
you felt gratitude towards them

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:55.460
and a bond to return
the favor in the future,

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:57.500
a drive to be pro-social.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:01.460
When you repaid them,
they felt gratitude towards you.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:04.440
This brought your ancestors
closer together

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.800
and forged bonds and friendships.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:10.920
So, early forms of gratitude
were biological mechanisms

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:13.860
that modified your behavior
towards cooperation,

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.720
which helped humans
to dominate Earth.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:17.560
But, over time,

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:20.940
gratitude became more than
just an impulse to play fair.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:24.060
2: The Consequences of Gratitude

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:29.060
Scientists found that gratitude
stimulates the pathways in your brain

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:31.060
involved in feelings of reward,

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:33.340
forming social bonds,

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:35.860
and interpreting other's intentions.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:39.900
It also makes it easier to save
and retrieve positive memories

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:45.100
Even more, gratitude directly counteracts
negative feelings and traits,

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:47.300
like envy and social comparison,

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:48.360
narcissism,

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:49.240
cynicism,

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:50.540
and materialism.

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:54.960
As a consequence, people who
are grateful, no matter what for,

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.360
tend to be happier and more satisfied.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.920
They have better relationships,

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:02.040
an easier time making friends.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:03.540
They sleep better,

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:07.280
tend to suffer less from
depression, addiction, and burnout,

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.980
and are better at dealing
with traumatic events.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:14.480
In a way, gratitude makes it
less likely that you'll fall into

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:17.320
one of the psychological traps
modern life has set for you.

00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:20.460
For example, gratitude
measurably counters

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:23.940
the tendency to forget and
downplay positive events.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:26.460
If you work long and
hard for something,

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:29.120
actually getting it can feel
daft and empty.

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:32.480
You can find yourself emotionally
back where you started

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:34.900
and try to achieve
the next biggest thing,

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:36.640
looking for that satisfaction,

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:38.860
instead of being satisfied with yourself.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:42.200
Or, imagine being lonely and
wanting to have more friends.

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:44.140
You actually might have someone

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:46.820
or even multiple people
who want to hang out,

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:48.940
but you might feel that
this is not enough,

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:51.300
that you're a loser and
feel bad about yourself.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:54.120
So you might turn down
their attempts to hang out

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:55.440
and become more lonely.

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:58.680
If you feel grateful for
your relationships instead,

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:00.320
you might accept invitations

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:01.900
or even take the initiative.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:04.260
The more often you risk opening up,

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:06.880
the higher the chance of
solidifying relationships

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:08.340
and meeting new people.

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:11.860
In the best case, gratitude
can trigger a feedback loop.

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:15.100
Positive feelings lead to
more pro-social behavior,

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:17.640
which leads to more
positive social experiences

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:19.680
that cause more
positive feelings.

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:23.300
This is a common experience
after serious hardship,

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:25.120
like chemotherapy, for example.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:28.320
Life can feel amazing
after a crisis is over.

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:31.860
The smallest things can be
bottomless sources of joy,

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:33.340
from being able to taste

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:36.080
to just sitting in the sun
or chatting with a friend.

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:41.460
Objectively, your life is the same or
maybe even slightly worse than before,

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:44.300
but your brain compares
your present experiences

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:46.200
with the times
when life was bad

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:47.920
and reacts with gratitude.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:50.260
So, in a nutshell,

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:54.220
gratitude refocuses your attention
towards the good things you have,

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:56.180
and the consequences of this shift

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:59.560
are better feelings and
more positive experiences.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.520
While it is great to
know these things,

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:05.480
is there actually a way for
you to feel more of it?

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:09.000
3: How To Make Your Brain More Grateful

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:13.560
The ability to experience
more or less gratitude

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:15.600
is not equally distributed.

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:18.280
You have what's known as
trait gratitude,

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:21.760
that determines how much
you are able to feel it.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:25.980
It depends on your genetics,
personality, and culture.

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:28.800
This discovery made
scientists wonder

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:32.400
if they could design exercises
that change your trait gratitude

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.120
and lead to more happiness.

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:36.920
Let's start with
important caveats.

00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:40.420
It's not yet entirely clear to what degree
gratitude can be trained

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:42.200
or how long the effects last.

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:44.620
There are no
magic pills for happiness.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:46.180
Life is complicated.

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:49.460
On some days, it feels like
you're in control of yourself,

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:51.860
and, on others, you
feel like you're not.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:53.160
And this is okay.

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:57.580
Also, sometimes pursuing happiness
can make you more unhappy

00:06:57.580 --> 00:06:59.780
if you put too much pressure on yourself.

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:04.080
Gratitude should also not be seen
as a solution to depression

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:06.300
or a substitute for professional help,.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:08.620
It can only be a piece of the puzzle.

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:10.900
It's not the solution to the puzzle itself.

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:16.040
The easiest gratitude exercise,
with the most solid research behind it,

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:17.540
is gratitude journaling.

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:19.840
It means sitting down
for a few minutes,

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:21.300
one to three times a week,

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:24.200
and writing down five to ten
things you're grateful for

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:26.600
It might feel weird at first,

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:27.960
so start simply.

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:30.920
Can you feel grateful
for a little thing?

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:32.460
Like how great coffee is,

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:34.100
or that someone
was kind to you.

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:37.400
Can you appreciate something
someone else did for you?

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:41.240
Can you reflect on which things or people
you would miss if they were gone

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:43.200
and be grateful that they're in your life?

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:45.160
We're all different,

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:47.100
so you'll know what
works for you.

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:49.000
And that's it, really.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:50.740
It feels almost insulting,

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:52.720
like things shouldn't
be that simple.

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:54.560
But in numerous studies,

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:56.760
the participants reported
more happiness

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:58.740
and a higher general
life satisfaction

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:00.980
after doing this practice
for a few weeks.

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:02.720
And, even more,

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:05.300
studies have found 
changes in brain activity

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:06.920
some months
after they ended.

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:11.380
Practicing gratitude may be
a real way to reprogram yourself.

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:15.140
This research shows that
your emotions are not fixed.

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:18.280
In the end, how
you experience life

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:21.100
is a representation of
what you believe about it.

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:24.680
If you attack your core beliefs
about yourself and your life,

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:26.740
you can change your
thoughts and feelings,

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:29.180
which automatically
changes your behavior.

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:33.200
It's pretty mind-blowing that
something as simple as self-reflection

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:36.640
can hack the pathways in our brain
to fight dissatisfaction.

00:08:37.420 --> 00:08:40.280
And, if this is no reason
to be more optimistic,

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:41.220
what is?

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:43.460
Being a human is hard,

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:45.460
but it doesn't need
to be as hard.

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:47.760
And, if you actively look,

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:51.220
you might find that your life is
much better than you thought.

00:08:51.620 --> 00:08:52.360
[Happy bark]

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:53.760
[Party horn]

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:56.380
If you're curious and
want to try out gratitude,

00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:57.300
we made a thing.

00:08:57.780 --> 00:09:01.840
Please note that you don't need to buy
anything from anyone to practice gratitude.

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:04.580
All you need is paper,
a pen, and five minutes.

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:08.660
Having said that, we've made
a Kurzgesagt gratitude journal,

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:10.220
based on studies we've read,

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:11.920
conversations with experts,

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:15.520
and our personal experiences
with gratitude over the last year.

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:18.700
It's structured in a way that
might make it a bit easier

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:20.860
to get into the habit of
gratitude journaling.

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:24.840
There are short explanations
and reflections to mix it up

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:26.360
and make it more interesting.

00:09:26.620 --> 00:09:28.800
We've also made it
as pretty as we could.

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:32.280
This video continues
the unofficial series

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:34.820
of more personal,
introspective videos,

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:38.340
from optimistic nihilism to
loneliness and now gratitude.

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:40.440
We don't want to be
a self-help channel,

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:43.540
so we'll keep this sort of video
at roughly one per year.

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:45.640
We hope they're helpful
to some of you.

00:09:46.340 --> 00:09:47.500
Thank you for watching.

